WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:07.230
- Hello, everyone, I'm Jeff Brock.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.240
I'm the Dean of the School of Engineering

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.310
and Applied Science.

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:14.100
We're going by the
moniker Yale Engineering,

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.380
just to drive the point home.

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:18.660
This is the Dean's Invited Speaker Series,

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.000
which I'm really pleased to inaugurate.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.430
We had the idea that
it would be a good way

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:26.160
to announce our new
strategy to the community

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.160
by inviting really affiliates,
friends from industry

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.160
that have taken their Yale
experience in some way

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.880
and turned it into a
bright, illustrious career

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:40.653
in industry in one way or another.

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:45.080
And our visitor today, Kathryn Guarini,

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.430
is an embodiment of this.

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:50.550
She has become a good
friend over a few meetings,

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:54.003
we have some commonalities
in our Yale experience.

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:58.290
Like me, she met her future spouse

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:01.500
while engaged in an
extracurricular activity,

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.480
in her case, The Yale Herald.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:07.143
In my case, I was moonlighting
in a James Joyce seminar.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:11.970
So be careful which extracurricular
activities you choose.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.490
You may end up reliving them

00:01:14.490 --> 00:01:16.143
throughout the rest of your life.

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:21.390
So, I'm also really excited

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:24.750
to have you hear about her story,

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:28.530
her trajectory through her
time working in industry,

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:31.050
and the different twists
and turns that's taken.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:34.800
But on a somewhat lighter
note, I'll just mention,

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.800
she has recently been mentioned
in as some top 10 ranking

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.910
in a list of 100 Most Influential Women

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:48.000
that included Taylor Swift,

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.900
Simone Biles, Beyonce,

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:53.816
and who's the other one?

00:01:53.816 --> 00:01:56.580
(Jeffrey laughs)

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.730
Yeah, anyway, so we're in the presence

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:01.350
of some celebrity here

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:05.470
and the School of Engineering
and Applied Science

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.030
in our Applied Physics
Department in particular,

00:02:09.030 --> 00:02:10.683
thank you Vidvuds for joining us,

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:15.000
generates a lot of celebrity in the world,

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.130
and Kathryn is among them.

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:18.990
So we're very pleased to welcome her.

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:22.230
She's gonna give about 30
minutes of presentation

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:24.150
and talk about her trajectory,

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:27.090
and then we'll have plenty
of time for some Q&amp;A,

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:28.920
which I'm happy to host.

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.275
So welcome, Kathryn, thank you for coming.

00:02:31.275 --> 00:02:34.442
(audience applauding)

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:39.090
- Hi, everyone. Thank you so much.

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:40.260
It's really great to be here.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.900
I graduated in 1994,

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.000
which sounds like a really,
really long time ago,

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:46.150
I'm sure to all of you.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:50.040
I was in Morse, I was an
applied physics major,

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:52.320
and I really have wonderful memories

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:53.370
of my time here at Yale,

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.350
so it's always nice to come back.

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:58.230
And I'm gonna share with
you a few things today.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:02.490
I have, let's see, three main topics,

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:04.435
and really this is meant
to be sort of a teaser

00:03:04.435 --> 00:03:07.350
of topics that I'm more
than happy to talk about.

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:08.600
So when we get to the Q&amp;A,

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:11.220
if there's anything that
you'd like to hear more about

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:15.330
or ask me questions, then
I'm happy to discuss.

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:17.490
But I wanted to first talk a
little bit about my career.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.560
What did I do when I left Yale

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.290
and how did that transpire

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:24.930
into a series of different opportunities?

00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:26.520
I think Yale really prepared me well,

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:29.370
but I had no idea where I would go next,

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:32.130
and I will tell you a little
bit about how that played out.

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:34.740
And then talk a bit about
technology and transformation

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:36.480
in my role at IBM.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:39.314
I spent 24 years in different capacities,

00:03:39.314 --> 00:03:42.210
largely trying to drive innovation

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:44.550
and transformation leveraging technology,

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:46.080
and I'll give you a little bit of a feel

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.750
for what that has looked like for me.

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:50.970
And then I wanna spend a few minutes

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:54.330
talking about the other
aspects of being a leader,

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:57.690
particularly a leader in
the technology industry.

00:03:57.690 --> 00:03:58.523
It would be great

00:03:58.523 --> 00:04:00.420
if we could only talk about
science and engineering,

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:03.540
but in reality that's fun and important,

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:06.198
but not sufficient to really drive impact.

00:04:06.198 --> 00:04:07.031
And so I think there are

00:04:07.031 --> 00:04:09.450
a lot of other aspects of leadership,

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:12.450
leading teams, driving execution,

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:16.710
managing crises, and others
that I learned on the job.

00:04:16.710 --> 00:04:18.420
And I think there's a real opportunity

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.390
to think through some of
that perhaps ahead of time

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:23.280
and be better prepared than I was

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.473
as I got into some of these experiences.

00:04:26.430 --> 00:04:29.193
So let's start with career first.

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:34.740
I imagine some of you
think of a career ladder

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:37.920
or some idea of some long-range plan.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:41.640
I always admired those
people who were really clear

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.160
about what they wanted
to do when they grew up.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:46.230
And I never knew.

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:48.750
In fact, while I was an
applied physics major,

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:50.430
if you had caught me in my first

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:52.290
or second year here at Yale,

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:53.700
I probably would've told you

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.550
that maybe I was going to be
a political science major,

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.310
or an Italian major, or art history,

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:01.260
or maybe I wanted to go into journalism,

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:03.390
or who knows, all sorts of things.

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.880
And then I ended up
focusing on applied physics

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:10.318
because it was hard, and I
liked solving hard problems,

00:05:10.318 --> 00:05:12.573
and I liked figuring things out.

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.710
My grandfather was a math
teacher here in New Haven,

00:05:16.710 --> 00:05:18.570
taught at the public schools,
Hillhouse High School,

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:20.070
for many, many years.

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:22.650
And for as long as I
can remember as a kid,

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:25.830
he thought doing math
was the best thing ever,

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:27.930
and he made me think that math as a tool

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:31.320
for solving problems was fun.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:34.020
And so when I got to actually apply it

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:36.510
to solving problems like in physics,

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:38.790
I thought that was fun too.

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:42.780
So after I got my degree
in applied physics,

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:44.520
well, actually before I got my degree,

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:46.560
I actually got a chance
to work as an intern

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:48.420
at Hewlett Packard out in California.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:51.100
And this was summer of 1993,

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:53.910
so it was sort of the
heyday of Silicon Valley.

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:57.570
And a professor here, Robert Wheeler,

00:05:57.570 --> 00:05:59.820
who I think is now retired,

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:02.910
but he connected me to
one of his former students

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:04.560
who was working at HP,

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:07.980
and that's how I ventured
out across the country

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:12.030
and spent the summer working
in industry in the tech world.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:14.880
Taking measurements in
a lab, writing code,

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:16.080
trying to figure out what you do

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:19.590
with an applied physics
degree in the real world.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:21.930
But what an experience
being in Silicon Valley,

00:06:21.930 --> 00:06:23.130
and I decided to go back.

00:06:23.130 --> 00:06:24.750
So I ended up going to Graduate School

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:28.800
at Stanford down the road from
HP in Palo Alto, California.

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:32.760
And being a part of the
Silicon Valley community

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:35.370
at that time was really stimulating.

00:06:35.370 --> 00:06:37.440
I went back to HP for another summer.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:40.650
I did an internship at
Advanced Micro Devices, or AMD,

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:44.070
who is now a tremendous
leader in the industry.

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:47.280
They were playing second
fiddle at the time to Intel

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:50.400
in semiconductor technology
and manufacturing,

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.830
but I got a chance to see what it was like

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:56.280
to take some of this science
that I learned in school

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:59.310
and actually apply it in the real world.

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:00.510
As a graduate student,

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:03.960
I worked with a professor
named Calvin Quate.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:05.670
He was about my grandfather's age.

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:08.580
I was among the very last
of the students he had.

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:12.210
He looked like Einstein,
big, white, crazy hair.

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:15.330
He was the co-inventor of
the acoustic microscope

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:17.550
and the atomic force microscope, or AFM,

00:07:17.550 --> 00:07:19.620
that some of you may know about.

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:20.700
And at the time,

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:24.900
everyone was talking about
the end of silicon scaling,

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:26.280
Moore's law,

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:28.350
that you wouldn't be able
to pattern using light

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:31.140
and using traditional optical
lithography any longer

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:34.200
to shrink the transistors
and scale the microchips.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:36.750
And therefore, we wouldn't
get faster computers,

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:38.610
we wouldn't be able to
have better electronics,

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:41.250
and there's lots of worry
at the time about this.

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:42.780
Now, world has changed,

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:46.410
and we still have faster computers
and powerful cell phones.

00:07:46.410 --> 00:07:48.030
But the time, everyone was thinking about:

00:07:48.030 --> 00:07:50.100
How can you scale the way

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:52.320
how small something you can pattern?

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:54.360
How do you make better transistors

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:55.530
that can actually turn off

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:59.580
when they get to the dimensions
around a few atoms thick?

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:01.020
And so that was what I did,

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:03.720
and I got to get in a
lab, build transistors,

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:07.890
use these scanning probes
to do parallel patterning,

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:09.750
emit electrons from a probe,

00:08:09.750 --> 00:08:12.450
build transistors, build chips, test them.

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:16.470
So it was a hugely
interesting experience, right?

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:19.596
Really trying to learn how
to actually build things

00:08:19.596 --> 00:08:24.596
and demonstrate value that
the industry was looking for.

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:29.040
So when I finished my degree,
I came directly to IBM,

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:32.730
and then I spent the next 24 years at IBM.

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:35.820
So a long time, but doing
so many different things.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:37.740
I started doing technical research.

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:40.650
So my first six or so years,
I was working in a lab,

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:42.210
and I was taking some of that experience

00:08:42.210 --> 00:08:43.440
I had in graduate school,

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:44.700
and doing internships,

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:47.490
and actually applying it to what IBM did,

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:50.430
which was to actually build
semiconductor technology

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:52.350
for their system's business,

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:54.180
building microprocessors

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:55.320
building all the chips

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:58.260
that were in the competing game stations,

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:00.570
the Nintendo chip, the Sony,

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:03.360
the Microsoft Xbox chip, and so on.

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:07.020
They needed to have high
performance technology

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:07.853
to deliver that.

00:09:07.853 --> 00:09:09.960
And so I first was in the research lab

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:12.810
experimenting with new transistor designs,

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:14.970
new structures, new materials.

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:17.340
And then I moved into the
development space, which is:

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:19.380
How do you get these to yield reliably?

00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:21.360
How can you build them at scale

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:23.536
and ensure that we have a business

00:09:23.536 --> 00:09:26.160
that is gonna be productive?

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:28.590
And then I got a chance
to move around the company

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:33.300
outside of that semiconductor
and research development space

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:35.310
because I wanted to know
what the rest of IBM did.

00:09:35.310 --> 00:09:37.320
And I'm gonna tell you
all a little bit about IBM

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:38.790
because I didn't know.

00:09:38.790 --> 00:09:41.130
All I knew was this little research world

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:42.810
because as a graduate student,

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:47.217
I'd read papers from researchers at IBM,

00:09:47.217 --> 00:09:49.440
and I thought I wanna do that.

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:52.020
But I didn't really know what
the rest of the company did.

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:55.140
So I deliberately moved
outside the Research Division,

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:57.360
got a chance to work in development

00:09:57.360 --> 00:09:59.430
of our microprocessor design.

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:01.110
I was managing a large global team

00:10:01.110 --> 00:10:02.460
designing microprocessors.

00:10:02.460 --> 00:10:03.810
Again, I'm not a circuit designer,

00:10:03.810 --> 00:10:06.060
but I was leading a team on that.

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:08.340
Moved into product management.

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:10.650
How do we launch net new products?

00:10:10.650 --> 00:10:12.270
We wanted to bring a technology.

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:14.070
The mainframe technology had been around

00:10:14.070 --> 00:10:15.960
for 50 years at that point.

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:17.490
We wanted to bring that technology

00:10:17.490 --> 00:10:22.490
known for reliable, secure,
highly available computing.

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:25.740
We wanted to bring that to new workloads

00:10:25.740 --> 00:10:30.240
and to new clients that had
never used a mainframe before.

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:31.260
How do you do that?

00:10:31.260 --> 00:10:34.050
Well, we actually
leveraged the open-source

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:36.999
operating system Linux on
the mainframe technology.

00:10:36.999 --> 00:10:40.830
We launched a new brand
we called LinuxONE.

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:42.427
We went out and talked
to clients and said,

00:10:42.427 --> 00:10:44.520
"How do we get you to use this product?

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:45.540
What is inhibiting you?

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:48.210
Is it skills? Is it user experience?

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:51.660
Is it functionality,
performance latency, et cetera?"

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:52.980
So bringing that back

00:10:52.980 --> 00:10:54.930
and then working with
our development teams

00:10:54.930 --> 00:10:57.810
to design the next
generation of that product.

00:10:57.810 --> 00:11:00.330
It's a completely different
type of experience for me.

00:11:00.330 --> 00:11:01.440
How do you price it?

00:11:01.440 --> 00:11:02.790
How do you go to market?

00:11:02.790 --> 00:11:04.230
Where's the product market fit?

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:05.700
Who is the competition?

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:08.880
It's a completely different
set of questions and answers.

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:11.850
And then I came back to
the Research Division

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:16.740
after having been outside of
that for almost a dozen years,

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:19.710
and I worked as the
Chief Operating Officer

00:11:19.710 --> 00:11:21.330
for the Research Division.

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:25.563
IBM Research has 3,000
researchers, largely PhD,

00:11:26.430 --> 00:11:28.770
mostly doing very deep technical work,

00:11:28.770 --> 00:11:32.820
some exploratory, more academic life-like,

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:35.100
and some very closely collaborating

00:11:35.100 --> 00:11:38.640
with our development teams
and that whole spectrum.

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:42.210
I've been around for 75
years, this Research Division,

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:46.110
but we were worried about
going the way of Bell Labs

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:49.020
and other industrial
research organizations

00:11:49.020 --> 00:11:50.550
that aren't around anymore.

00:11:50.550 --> 00:11:53.130
And so my role as the COO was:

00:11:53.130 --> 00:11:54.960
How do you make sure we're relevant?

00:11:54.960 --> 00:11:56.490
How do we increase the impact

00:11:56.490 --> 00:12:00.210
but not lose that very special capability

00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:02.610
that was able to have Nobel Prize winners

00:12:02.610 --> 00:12:04.650
and to have very, very high recognition

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:07.440
for the innovation in
the Research Division?

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:11.490
And then I spent the last
2 1/2 years as IBM's CIO,

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:13.200
which is the Chief Information Officer,

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:16.140
and I'll tell you a little
bit about what that role is

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:17.910
because again, I didn't really know.

00:12:17.910 --> 00:12:20.630
I didn't really know what
the role was all about.

00:12:20.630 --> 00:12:23.880
And it's different in
different organizations.

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:27.330
But suffice it to say, large organization,

00:12:27.330 --> 00:12:31.050
big set of deliverables that
the company was relying on.

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:32.190
And I got a chance

00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:34.560
to use a lot of the skills I had developed

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:36.360
over the course of my career,

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:39.000
to figure out how do you
solve both technical problems

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:42.060
but also some of the
execution and leadership

00:12:42.060 --> 00:12:45.540
and other aspects of leading in innovation

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:47.460
when you have high stakes.

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:51.270
Because at that point, all IBM
employees and all IBM clients

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:53.550
were then relying on the CIO team

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:57.513
to deliver the IT and the
capabilities to do their jobs.

00:12:58.590 --> 00:13:02.580
So, I can go forward here.

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:04.860
Let me just tell you
real briefly about IBM.

00:13:04.860 --> 00:13:06.740
So it was founded in 1911,

00:13:06.740 --> 00:13:09.030
so it's been around a very, very long time

00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:09.960
and it's changed a lot.

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:11.250
You see at the bottom of the chart

00:13:11.250 --> 00:13:13.350
some of the things it's known for,

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:17.040
from punch cards, to computing machines,

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:19.860
helping to land people on the moon,

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:22.020
certainly well known for that.

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:27.020
Watson beating "Jeopardy!"
in 2011 was a proud moment.

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:28.710
I started to kind of understand

00:13:28.710 --> 00:13:30.300
what natural language processing

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:34.170
and question-answering systems could do.

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:36.720
And today, the the
flagship product from IBM

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:37.553
is called Watsonx,

00:13:37.553 --> 00:13:40.620
and you may have seen some
advertising about that recently.

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:42.600
But what's interesting when
I'll tell you a little about

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:47.010
the IT or the CIO role is the
size and scale of the company.

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:50.580
There are 280,000 employees at IBM,

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:52.560
it's like the size of a small country.

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:57.210
But it does business in 170
plus countries of the world,

00:13:57.210 --> 00:14:00.030
which means you have to deal
with different cultures,

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:04.830
languages, regulatory
requirements, laws, et cetera,

00:14:04.830 --> 00:14:06.990
and time zones when you're managing

00:14:06.990 --> 00:14:10.080
and supporting that community.

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:11.700
And then the size and
scale of the business,

00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:15.000
it's about a $60 billion revenue annually.

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:18.540
And it has three main
portions of the business.

00:14:18.540 --> 00:14:21.510
It still has what's legacy infrastructure,

00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:23.850
hardware business, delivering systems.

00:14:23.850 --> 00:14:26.370
Was largely what I had
started in that space.

00:14:26.370 --> 00:14:30.240
It has a very growing software
portion of the business.

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:31.350
And the big piece of that

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:33.990
was the acquisition of
the company Red Hat.

00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:37.290
It's known for open source
enterprise software.

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:39.360
And then there's a consulting business.

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:41.670
So technology and business services

00:14:41.670 --> 00:14:43.473
that IBM supports as well.

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:47.820
And so as the CIO,

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:52.020
I was the global person and
organization responsible

00:14:52.020 --> 00:14:57.020
for delivering IT to that IBM community.

00:14:57.960 --> 00:15:02.960
So large number of people,
lots of geographic spread,

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:06.630
and working with clients in
all these different industries.

00:15:06.630 --> 00:15:08.010
So this is a quick picture

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:11.850
of what the CIO organization
at IBM is responsible for,

00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:13.620
and I'll point you to this middle section.

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:15.480
It's called Digital Workplace Solutions.

00:15:15.480 --> 00:15:19.140
It's mostly what people
associate with IT or a CIO.

00:15:19.140 --> 00:15:22.800
You get your workstation,
your laptop, mobile device.

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:27.281
You get on the intranet that
can get IT help or support.

00:15:27.281 --> 00:15:29.700
And those certainly are the things,

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:30.870
one of some of the things

00:15:30.870 --> 00:15:33.960
that I and the team was responsible for.

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:37.500
But little did I know, the stuff on top,

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:41.010
the Business Applications
that actually run the company

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:43.080
were actually the lion's
share of the investment

00:15:43.080 --> 00:15:45.660
and the complexity that
we were responsible for.

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:49.710
So running payroll
systems, and procurement,

00:15:49.710 --> 00:15:54.270
and real estate, and supply chain,

00:15:54.270 --> 00:15:57.090
all the HR systems, et cetera, finance,

00:15:57.090 --> 00:15:59.160
how do you close the books.

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:03.480
All these applications, over
4,000 enterprise applications,

00:16:03.480 --> 00:16:05.130
that we needed to run,

00:16:05.130 --> 00:16:10.130
IBM successfully need to be
reliable, secure, scalable

00:16:10.200 --> 00:16:12.630
that when a client has an issue,

00:16:12.630 --> 00:16:14.010
they can get in touch with

00:16:14.010 --> 00:16:16.413
and get response times immediately.

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:19.080
How do you modernize that environment?

00:16:19.080 --> 00:16:21.540
How do we take advantage
of new technologies?

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:23.850
How do we improve the
experience for our employees

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:24.873
and for our clients,

00:16:24.873 --> 00:16:27.120
that we transform this

00:16:27.120 --> 00:16:29.550
and reduce how much we're
spending while we're doing that?

00:16:29.550 --> 00:16:31.650
So that was this challenge

00:16:31.650 --> 00:16:34.290
and getting our arms around
that and maintaining it.

00:16:34.290 --> 00:16:35.220
And then, of course,

00:16:35.220 --> 00:16:37.140
we're responsible for the infrastructure

00:16:37.140 --> 00:16:39.123
on which that is all run.

00:16:40.140 --> 00:16:42.990
And because IBM's an IT company,

00:16:42.990 --> 00:16:44.520
I wanted to be an early adopter

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:46.230
of all IBM's technology, right?

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:49.470
So just as we develop a new server system,

00:16:49.470 --> 00:16:51.090
or a new piece of software,

00:16:51.090 --> 00:16:53.490
or we have a way of partnering

00:16:53.490 --> 00:16:57.210
with a set of strategic
technologies in the industry,

00:16:57.210 --> 00:17:01.050
I wanted to be the very
first to use that technology

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:02.940
so that we could demonstrate the value.

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:04.607
If it can work for IBM at our scale,

00:17:04.607 --> 00:17:07.920
it can work for any one of IBM's clients,

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:09.480
and then we can also provide feedback

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:12.360
to our development and our
product teams to make it better

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:14.280
so that IBM could grow as a business.

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:15.960
So we had both the responsibilities

00:17:15.960 --> 00:17:17.970
sort of at the back office
to support the company,

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:20.310
but also to be out there leading edge

00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:21.990
to make sure that we're taking advantage

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:23.490
of IBM's technology

00:17:23.490 --> 00:17:25.200
'cause you can imagine
if you go to a customer

00:17:25.200 --> 00:17:28.500
and you say, "IBM, we're
focused on hybrid cloud, and AI,

00:17:28.500 --> 00:17:32.100
and Watsonx, we've got this
great stuff, you, CIO..."

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:34.897
Because IBM actually sells to CIOs

00:17:34.897 --> 00:17:38.737
in the industry of banks, and
retail companies, and so on.

00:17:38.737 --> 00:17:40.287
"We've got a solution for you?"

00:17:41.550 --> 00:17:44.183
What do you think the first
thing that they're gonna ask is?

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:46.800
Do you use it yourself?

00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:48.600
Does it work for you?

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:52.230
And if the answer's no, we
lose all of our credibility.

00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:54.900
And so it's really important
that we be out there in front.

00:17:54.900 --> 00:17:56.220
It means we're taking risk,

00:17:56.220 --> 00:17:57.840
that means we're being an early adopter

00:17:57.840 --> 00:17:59.133
and actually co-creating.

00:18:01.590 --> 00:18:03.720
All right, so I wanna talk a
little bit about technology.

00:18:03.720 --> 00:18:06.270
I'll just let you read
this little cartoon.

00:18:06.270 --> 00:18:07.520
It's one of my favorites.

00:18:12.240 --> 00:18:15.270
Everyone's so excited about
ChatGPT, and it's wonderful.

00:18:15.270 --> 00:18:19.140
I mean, how exciting that we
all have AI in our hands today,

00:18:19.140 --> 00:18:20.580
it really is,

00:18:20.580 --> 00:18:23.940
but that's not the same as
applying AI or any technology

00:18:23.940 --> 00:18:27.870
to large-scale enterprise environments

00:18:27.870 --> 00:18:30.660
'cause I have to worry about data privacy

00:18:30.660 --> 00:18:32.280
and confidentiality.

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:34.800
I have to worry about
scale and repeatability.

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:35.820
Is it trustworthy?

00:18:35.820 --> 00:18:37.533
What data was it trained on?

00:18:38.490 --> 00:18:41.010
Can I explain the results?

00:18:41.010 --> 00:18:43.770
And so I want to use
all of the technologies

00:18:43.770 --> 00:18:45.960
in the very best there is,

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:48.420
but I need to be very
thoughtful and responsible

00:18:48.420 --> 00:18:50.400
about the applications of that technology.

00:18:50.400 --> 00:18:52.650
And it's not technology and innovation

00:18:52.650 --> 00:18:55.680
just for technology and innovation's sake.

00:18:55.680 --> 00:18:57.480
It's about: What problems are we solving

00:18:57.480 --> 00:18:59.760
and what value are we delivering?

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:03.180
So I always like to start
with what are the issues

00:19:03.180 --> 00:19:07.290
or what are the concerns on
the minds of business leaders

00:19:07.290 --> 00:19:08.250
in an enterprise?

00:19:08.250 --> 00:19:11.910
And so here are some, there
are, of course, many more,

00:19:11.910 --> 00:19:14.520
but huge pressure at an enterprise level.

00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:17.460
And I saw this at IBM and I
saw this in our clients, right?

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:20.527
Most of our clients are coming
to a company like IBM saying,

00:19:20.527 --> 00:19:24.240
"Help us reinvent
ourselves using technology;

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:26.700
new business models, new
ways of going to market,

00:19:26.700 --> 00:19:29.130
new ways of developing products."

00:19:29.130 --> 00:19:32.640
Productivity is the word of the year,

00:19:32.640 --> 00:19:34.530
of the decade, perhaps.

00:19:34.530 --> 00:19:37.770
Most CEOs, so the leaders
of these companies,

00:19:37.770 --> 00:19:42.090
are telling us that what they
care about is productivity.

00:19:42.090 --> 00:19:43.800
And what that usually translates into,

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:46.560
how can I be more efficient and effective?

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:47.760
How can I get my workforce

00:19:47.760 --> 00:19:50.430
to get more output for a given input?

00:19:50.430 --> 00:19:51.870
How do I get more things done?

00:19:51.870 --> 00:19:55.290
And so AI and technologies
are certain a piece of that,

00:19:55.290 --> 00:19:57.330
but it's also new ways
of working the hybrid

00:19:57.330 --> 00:19:58.590
or remote working model.

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:02.790
It's labor shortages, it's
skill shifts, it's a huge focus,

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:04.800
and technology can play a key role.

00:20:04.800 --> 00:20:07.440
Cybersecurity is a huge concern.

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:09.840
Massive number, and frequency,

00:20:09.840 --> 00:20:12.930
and severity of cyber attacks today

00:20:12.930 --> 00:20:15.210
that are very, very costly
not just in dollars,

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:18.660
but reputational cost, and
time, and business value.

00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:21.210
So we need to protect against that.

00:20:21.210 --> 00:20:23.698
Sustainability has become top of mind,

00:20:23.698 --> 00:20:25.950
probably should have been
for a very long time.

00:20:25.950 --> 00:20:29.550
But it now finally is clear that all of us

00:20:29.550 --> 00:20:34.410
and businesses are playing a
role in impacting our climate,

00:20:34.410 --> 00:20:35.880
and we need to take responsibility

00:20:35.880 --> 00:20:37.620
for being very, very thoughtful.

00:20:37.620 --> 00:20:40.320
And technology is both a contributor,

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:42.720
as well as a potential solution in:

00:20:42.720 --> 00:20:45.570
How do we address some of
the pressing challenges

00:20:45.570 --> 00:20:47.100
around sustainability?

00:20:47.100 --> 00:20:49.830
Geopolitical risks,
this is rising threats.

00:20:49.830 --> 00:20:53.880
This is supply chain
vulnerabilities and much more.

00:20:53.880 --> 00:20:55.290
And finally, public health,

00:20:55.290 --> 00:20:56.910
and we've just been through a pandemic.

00:20:56.910 --> 00:20:59.760
But it's not only infectious
diseases, it's mental health.

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:02.430
And it's so many other
aspects of public health.

00:21:02.430 --> 00:21:04.410
Care about that at an enterprise level

00:21:04.410 --> 00:21:07.260
'cause it's our employees,
it's our clients,

00:21:07.260 --> 00:21:09.000
and society at large.

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:12.030
And so if you think about these are a set

00:21:12.030 --> 00:21:16.770
of the problems that we see in
front of us every single day,

00:21:16.770 --> 00:21:20.280
technology and innovation can play a role

00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:21.150
in addressing this.

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:23.010
But it's not just about:

00:21:23.010 --> 00:21:25.203
How many applications of AI do you have?

00:21:26.160 --> 00:21:29.670
Interesting, but more
important about the, "So what?"

00:21:29.670 --> 00:21:32.190
What are you doing with
it that's meaningful

00:21:32.190 --> 00:21:35.253
and impactful to the company
and to the organization?

00:21:36.330 --> 00:21:38.400
So I'll highlight just three technologies

00:21:38.400 --> 00:21:41.160
that I think are important today.

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:42.630
There, of course, are many, many more,

00:21:42.630 --> 00:21:46.470
and these are in different
levels of maturity.

00:21:46.470 --> 00:21:49.290
I'd say hybrid cloud or
cloud computing today

00:21:49.290 --> 00:21:53.700
is very much a mature and
available set of capabilities,

00:21:53.700 --> 00:21:56.730
but still in its infancy in
terms of wide-scale adoption.

00:21:56.730 --> 00:21:58.600
And by that, we largely mean

00:21:59.460 --> 00:22:03.060
the legacy world was all
these distributed data centers

00:22:03.060 --> 00:22:03.900
all over the world.

00:22:03.900 --> 00:22:05.760
When I took on the job of CIO,

00:22:05.760 --> 00:22:08.760
there were 74 different data centers

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:11.550
where IBM was running workload

00:22:11.550 --> 00:22:14.790
for our internal use all over the world.

00:22:14.790 --> 00:22:15.623
I mean, I think about it like,

00:22:15.623 --> 00:22:17.520
"How do I get my arms around that?

00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:18.960
Where is everything running?

00:22:18.960 --> 00:22:20.640
How do I secure it?

00:22:20.640 --> 00:22:22.230
How do I think about the sustainability?

00:22:22.230 --> 00:22:24.750
What's the carbon footprint of all that?

00:22:24.750 --> 00:22:27.990
How do I manage latency and
performance and user experience?

00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:31.830
How do I modernize something
that is that fragmented?"

00:22:31.830 --> 00:22:34.500
And so by moving to a true hybrid cloud,

00:22:34.500 --> 00:22:37.710
and by that we mean a
combination of on-prem, private,

00:22:37.710 --> 00:22:38.880
and public cloud,

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:42.090
not one public cloud answer only

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:44.220
'cause that typically
locks you into a solution

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:47.100
that may not be any better
than you were before.

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:48.930
But having some flexibility

00:22:48.930 --> 00:22:51.870
to be able to move workloads
across different environments.

00:22:51.870 --> 00:22:55.980
If I maybe have a change in
the requirement from a client

00:22:55.980 --> 00:22:57.450
or from an employee,

00:22:57.450 --> 00:22:59.550
I wanna be able to have more flexibility,

00:22:59.550 --> 00:23:02.580
which I didn't have in the
old legacy environments.

00:23:02.580 --> 00:23:05.250
We've moved a huge
amount of IBM's workload

00:23:05.250 --> 00:23:06.900
into this more flexible environment,

00:23:06.900 --> 00:23:08.280
and we're seeing tremendous benefits

00:23:08.280 --> 00:23:11.400
in terms of how quickly we
can deploy applications,

00:23:11.400 --> 00:23:13.650
how much money we save,

00:23:13.650 --> 00:23:16.233
and frankly, the carbon
footprint piece as well.

00:23:17.070 --> 00:23:19.080
AI, I mean this is the
hottest topic today,

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:20.730
I am sure everybody here
is talking about it,

00:23:20.730 --> 00:23:23.910
thinking about it, using
it, which is great.

00:23:23.910 --> 00:23:26.460
As I said, it's really
about the responsible use

00:23:26.460 --> 00:23:27.990
of that technology.

00:23:27.990 --> 00:23:31.200
But we have a number of
enterprise applications,

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:34.830
in particular we've used robotic
process automation, or RPA,

00:23:34.830 --> 00:23:37.260
to automate manual tasks.

00:23:37.260 --> 00:23:39.930
This isn't about displacing workers,

00:23:39.930 --> 00:23:44.010
it's actually about getting
off of their plates.

00:23:44.010 --> 00:23:46.080
A lot of the more mundane tasks

00:23:46.080 --> 00:23:47.730
and freeing their time and energy up

00:23:47.730 --> 00:23:49.440
to do higher value work,

00:23:49.440 --> 00:23:51.480
which is important, and needed,

00:23:51.480 --> 00:23:54.900
and frankly valued by
our employees as well.

00:23:54.900 --> 00:23:56.190
We had a number of applications,

00:23:56.190 --> 00:24:01.190
like automating HR and
IT support using AI.

00:24:02.310 --> 00:24:05.610
So you don't have to wait on a call center

00:24:05.610 --> 00:24:07.740
and worrying about what
hours is there someone there.

00:24:07.740 --> 00:24:11.040
We're used to this, sorry,
in the consumer world.

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:12.930
But many enterprises don't have it

00:24:12.930 --> 00:24:15.690
because they worry about
security and privacy

00:24:15.690 --> 00:24:16.830
in many of their aspects.

00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:21.720
But at this point, if I wanna
transfer an employee at IBM,

00:24:21.720 --> 00:24:23.760
or do a salary increase,

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:26.340
or do a whole host of other HR tasks,

00:24:26.340 --> 00:24:28.440
they can almost exclusively be done

00:24:28.440 --> 00:24:31.787
through digital assistant
chatbot AI technology.

00:24:31.787 --> 00:24:33.240
It can be done 24 hours a day,

00:24:33.240 --> 00:24:35.520
it can be done with inline
language translation

00:24:35.520 --> 00:24:36.480
if you're in a part of the world

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:38.370
and you don't speak English,
you're not comfortable.

00:24:38.370 --> 00:24:40.140
It gives you a lot more flexibility,

00:24:40.140 --> 00:24:42.780
and we just applied some
very similar technology

00:24:42.780 --> 00:24:45.450
to providing that IT support.

00:24:45.450 --> 00:24:46.500
And quantum computing.

00:24:46.500 --> 00:24:50.250
So Yale also is very,
very big in this space.

00:24:50.250 --> 00:24:51.573
IBM has invested.

00:24:53.190 --> 00:24:54.450
This is what we have today,

00:24:54.450 --> 00:24:55.950
but I think there's still opportunity,

00:24:55.950 --> 00:24:58.110
very much opportunity into the future

00:24:58.110 --> 00:25:00.900
on how do we leverage
new computing technology

00:25:00.900 --> 00:25:02.160
not to take over,

00:25:02.160 --> 00:25:05.520
not to replace traditional
classical computing,

00:25:05.520 --> 00:25:07.290
but to be able to solve some problems

00:25:07.290 --> 00:25:09.150
that simply can't be solved

00:25:09.150 --> 00:25:11.310
with the technology that we have today?

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:12.933
It's very exciting.

00:25:14.090 --> 00:25:15.755
The work that IBM,

00:25:15.755 --> 00:25:18.690
in collaboration with
a number of partners,

00:25:18.690 --> 00:25:21.564
is advancing this capability very fast,

00:25:21.564 --> 00:25:24.420
and we're seeing that now
people can actually solve

00:25:24.420 --> 00:25:25.620
some really interesting problems,

00:25:25.620 --> 00:25:27.750
starting to see quantum advantage.

00:25:27.750 --> 00:25:31.530
The number, this is 433 cubits
or bits that are available

00:25:31.530 --> 00:25:35.010
for quantum computation today

00:25:35.010 --> 00:25:37.770
with a commitment by the end
of the year will be 1,000,

00:25:37.770 --> 00:25:39.930
and before long tens of thousands.

00:25:39.930 --> 00:25:40.800
I mean, that's a term...

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:42.300
I honestly, truly didn't think

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:44.310
that that was gonna be
possible in our lifetime.

00:25:44.310 --> 00:25:47.010
It's an amazing level of progress,

00:25:47.010 --> 00:25:48.450
but it's interesting on the technology.

00:25:48.450 --> 00:25:49.590
But then what do you do with it?

00:25:49.590 --> 00:25:53.490
So the applications, the use
cases in quantum chemistry,

00:25:53.490 --> 00:25:57.840
in finance, and optimization
are really compelling.

00:25:57.840 --> 00:26:02.160
And the network of universities, startups,

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:06.000
and large Fortune 500 companies
that are starting to explore

00:26:06.000 --> 00:26:08.760
what quantum computing might do for them,

00:26:08.760 --> 00:26:10.260
it's very exciting.

00:26:10.260 --> 00:26:14.250
There's also the fear
factor, which is, "Oh, gosh,

00:26:14.250 --> 00:26:17.460
when we have large
scale quantum computing,

00:26:17.460 --> 00:26:18.900
this will be able to break

00:26:18.900 --> 00:26:21.900
traditional public key cryptography."

00:26:21.900 --> 00:26:23.220
And that's true,

00:26:23.220 --> 00:26:26.070
but there are new cryptographic algorithms

00:26:26.070 --> 00:26:27.540
like lattice cryptography

00:26:27.540 --> 00:26:30.870
that are robust against
large-scale quantum computing.

00:26:30.870 --> 00:26:35.250
But then that takes us back
to our cybersecurity focus,

00:26:35.250 --> 00:26:38.010
and what do we do to make sure
that we're really prepared

00:26:38.010 --> 00:26:40.680
for quantum computing,
take advantage of it,

00:26:40.680 --> 00:26:44.310
but we also protect
against some of the risks

00:26:44.310 --> 00:26:46.743
that any technology brings?

00:26:49.410 --> 00:26:53.010
All right, and then my last
section is about leadership,

00:26:53.010 --> 00:26:54.690
and one of my favorite cartoons here

00:26:54.690 --> 00:26:56.580
because I think it's so telling.

00:26:56.580 --> 00:27:00.480
Most people think, "Oh,
great, you're the CIO?

00:27:00.480 --> 00:27:02.697
So you just tell everybody what to do."

00:27:03.900 --> 00:27:05.700
That must be pretty
straightforward, right?

00:27:05.700 --> 00:27:06.690
Jeff, you're the dean, right?

00:27:06.690 --> 00:27:09.168
You just get to tell everybody
what to do, right? Really...

00:27:09.168 --> 00:27:11.190
(Kathryn and audience laughing)

00:27:11.190 --> 00:27:12.690
And that would be nice,

00:27:12.690 --> 00:27:15.270
but the reality is, and you all know this,

00:27:15.270 --> 00:27:18.180
leadership is messy, and it's complicated,

00:27:18.180 --> 00:27:20.550
and it's challenging in many ways.

00:27:20.550 --> 00:27:22.890
And so I thought I would highlight

00:27:22.890 --> 00:27:24.900
some of the aspects of leading,

00:27:24.900 --> 00:27:26.670
whether you're leading an IT team,

00:27:26.670 --> 00:27:29.010
or a product development organization,

00:27:29.010 --> 00:27:31.290
or research community,

00:27:31.290 --> 00:27:34.950
or launching net new products
that you need to think about

00:27:34.950 --> 00:27:38.040
that are outside of the bits and bytes,

00:27:38.040 --> 00:27:40.590
outside of the science,

00:27:40.590 --> 00:27:44.220
but are critically important to success.

00:27:44.220 --> 00:27:48.120
So the first is, when you're
driving these projects,

00:27:48.120 --> 00:27:50.730
there's a whole host of
things that we do as leaders

00:27:50.730 --> 00:27:51.900
that need to be thoughtful about:

00:27:51.900 --> 00:27:54.450
What does success look like?

00:27:54.450 --> 00:27:57.120
What are we really trying to achieve?

00:27:57.120 --> 00:27:59.100
And how will we know

00:27:59.100 --> 00:28:02.910
we're making the necessary
progress to deliver?

00:28:02.910 --> 00:28:05.970
So there's the strategy
aspect, the project planning,

00:28:05.970 --> 00:28:07.860
the budgeting, the prioritization,

00:28:07.860 --> 00:28:10.173
what guardrails do you have in place?

00:28:11.100 --> 00:28:15.270
What metrics and measurements,
what governance do we have?

00:28:15.270 --> 00:28:19.320
And this stuff is not
all that straightforward.

00:28:19.320 --> 00:28:23.610
It depends a lot on the team,
and the priorities, and how,

00:28:23.610 --> 00:28:25.590
but hugely important to success.

00:28:25.590 --> 00:28:27.600
And again, it almost doesn't matter

00:28:27.600 --> 00:28:29.190
what you're trying to do.

00:28:29.190 --> 00:28:31.710
You're trying to deliver
a new mainframe system

00:28:31.710 --> 00:28:32.610
to the market,

00:28:32.610 --> 00:28:34.770
you're developing quantum computing,

00:28:34.770 --> 00:28:37.620
you're trying to create
an innovation through AI

00:28:37.620 --> 00:28:41.940
and delivering it to
280,000 IBM employees.

00:28:41.940 --> 00:28:44.430
You have to be clear on
what does success look like

00:28:44.430 --> 00:28:45.263
and how do you ensure

00:28:45.263 --> 00:28:47.253
that you can deliver on that commitment.

00:28:48.210 --> 00:28:51.300
But there are always risks, always.

00:28:51.300 --> 00:28:53.640
So how are you thoughtful and proactive

00:28:53.640 --> 00:28:56.400
about identifying what those risks are?

00:28:56.400 --> 00:28:59.370
And how do you minimize those risks,

00:28:59.370 --> 00:29:02.520
but also prepare for the eventualities

00:29:02.520 --> 00:29:03.960
of when something goes wrong?

00:29:03.960 --> 00:29:05.643
'Cause it always does, right?

00:29:07.110 --> 00:29:09.540
Have all sorts of crises
stories that I could tell,

00:29:09.540 --> 00:29:12.450
but I will tell you one that
will probably make you laugh.

00:29:12.450 --> 00:29:15.030
We moved to a new mail
system a couple years ago.

00:29:15.030 --> 00:29:16.200
And as the CIO,

00:29:16.200 --> 00:29:18.960
it was one of the things
that I was responsible for,

00:29:18.960 --> 00:29:19.890
one of many things,

00:29:19.890 --> 00:29:23.910
and didn't think it would
raise to the top level

00:29:23.910 --> 00:29:26.730
of my focus and attention.

00:29:26.730 --> 00:29:29.970
But when you move 280,000
employees to new mail system

00:29:29.970 --> 00:29:30.960
and the system goes down

00:29:30.960 --> 00:29:34.860
and they can't access their
email for upwards of two weeks,

00:29:34.860 --> 00:29:36.210
that's a crisis.

00:29:36.210 --> 00:29:37.113
Made the paper.

00:29:38.040 --> 00:29:42.180
I had to record all
sorts of communications

00:29:42.180 --> 00:29:44.130
to the entire global community,

00:29:44.130 --> 00:29:47.130
to talk to our CEO on a daily basis.

00:29:47.130 --> 00:29:49.364
We had to put an action plan.

00:29:49.364 --> 00:29:51.090
It wasn't something we
could just switch a button

00:29:51.090 --> 00:29:52.650
and be back.

00:29:52.650 --> 00:29:54.750
There were technical challenges,

00:29:54.750 --> 00:29:55.980
there were leadership challenges,

00:29:55.980 --> 00:29:57.930
there were communication challenges,

00:29:57.930 --> 00:30:00.150
there was root cause analysis
that we needed to understand.

00:30:00.150 --> 00:30:03.930
And then once we got
ourselves out of that crisis,

00:30:03.930 --> 00:30:05.490
we needed to make sure that we were clear

00:30:05.490 --> 00:30:08.040
how we would prevent anything like that

00:30:08.040 --> 00:30:09.420
from happening again.

00:30:09.420 --> 00:30:11.370
So how do you learn from that experience,

00:30:11.370 --> 00:30:13.920
and not just Kathryn learning

00:30:13.920 --> 00:30:16.140
or not just the leader
of the project learning,

00:30:16.140 --> 00:30:17.640
but the organization at large, right?

00:30:17.640 --> 00:30:19.740
How do you help the
organization to be better

00:30:19.740 --> 00:30:21.903
for any of the issues and concerns?

00:30:22.860 --> 00:30:25.770
And then perhaps the most important thing,

00:30:25.770 --> 00:30:28.920
and if you even remember
one thing from my story,

00:30:28.920 --> 00:30:33.210
it's all about the people
because you can do great science

00:30:33.210 --> 00:30:35.310
and you can have interesting project plans

00:30:35.310 --> 00:30:39.000
and crisis management proposals,

00:30:39.000 --> 00:30:42.270
but it's all about the
people that you have

00:30:42.270 --> 00:30:46.320
to get your projects done, and to inspire,

00:30:46.320 --> 00:30:49.650
and to drive innovation,
and to do delivery.

00:30:49.650 --> 00:30:54.650
And those people, they
require handholding,

00:30:54.660 --> 00:30:58.740
and support, and
inspiration, and leadership

00:30:58.740 --> 00:31:00.990
in order for them to be most effective.

00:31:00.990 --> 00:31:03.330
How do you build effective teams?

00:31:03.330 --> 00:31:06.480
How do you build a a
multidisciplinary set of capabilities,

00:31:06.480 --> 00:31:08.370
people with different expertise

00:31:08.370 --> 00:31:11.940
who understand the different
cultures, and geographies,

00:31:11.940 --> 00:31:14.340
and laws, and disciplines?

00:31:14.340 --> 00:31:16.680
And how do you help them to
work together effectively

00:31:16.680 --> 00:31:19.860
if they have these different
perspectives and experiences?

00:31:19.860 --> 00:31:23.850
How do you reward or
incent the right behavior?

00:31:23.850 --> 00:31:28.740
How do you provide performance
feedback effectively coaching

00:31:28.740 --> 00:31:31.590
and you manage your team effectively?

00:31:31.590 --> 00:31:35.640
All of this is sort of not
taught in school, I would say,

00:31:35.640 --> 00:31:40.220
but so important to the success
of any of these projects.

00:31:40.220 --> 00:31:45.000
In my experience, what
we tend to not recognize

00:31:45.000 --> 00:31:46.950
or even prioritize enough,

00:31:46.950 --> 00:31:50.370
even from those of us who
have risen through the ranks,

00:31:50.370 --> 00:31:54.180
who have a PhD, and vice
president experience,

00:31:54.180 --> 00:31:56.520
and leading large organizations,

00:31:56.520 --> 00:31:59.430
because I'll tell you the first
time I had a 500-person team

00:31:59.430 --> 00:32:01.580
and we're trying to
build a microprocessor,

00:32:02.490 --> 00:32:04.950
and you have billions of
billions of transistors

00:32:04.950 --> 00:32:06.780
that all need to be connected together

00:32:06.780 --> 00:32:08.910
in a way that you can tape this out on us,

00:32:08.910 --> 00:32:11.133
specific date, 18 months from now.

00:32:13.020 --> 00:32:16.980
Well now, you have the complexity
of managing that schedule,

00:32:16.980 --> 00:32:19.590
you have the technical
hurdles that need to get done.

00:32:19.590 --> 00:32:21.090
How are you gonna have the right latency,

00:32:21.090 --> 00:32:23.640
and performance, and so on?

00:32:23.640 --> 00:32:25.530
You have the personnel aspects.

00:32:25.530 --> 00:32:27.900
Do you have the right
skills and capabilities,

00:32:27.900 --> 00:32:29.400
roles and responsibilities?

00:32:29.400 --> 00:32:31.230
Who's gonna be making the decisions?

00:32:31.230 --> 00:32:34.110
What happens when all of
a sudden, six months in,

00:32:34.110 --> 00:32:36.840
there's a new opportunity or challenge?

00:32:36.840 --> 00:32:38.280
There's a competitive issue.

00:32:38.280 --> 00:32:40.380
There's a technical issue.

00:32:40.380 --> 00:32:44.294
You have to have these kinds
of experiences and skills

00:32:44.294 --> 00:32:45.390
in your pocket,

00:32:45.390 --> 00:32:47.100
so you can pull them out and respond.

00:32:47.100 --> 00:32:48.810
And if you don't, you learn on the job,

00:32:48.810 --> 00:32:51.630
and you find mentors and you
find others who can help you

00:32:51.630 --> 00:32:53.070
to be effective.

00:32:53.070 --> 00:32:55.788
But for sure that you don't do the things

00:32:55.788 --> 00:32:59.340
that I've been talking about with science

00:32:59.340 --> 00:33:01.290
or with engineering alone.

00:33:01.290 --> 00:33:02.400
It's super important.

00:33:02.400 --> 00:33:03.510
I think the credibility

00:33:03.510 --> 00:33:06.330
of understanding the
science and engineering

00:33:06.330 --> 00:33:07.860
to many of these leadership positions

00:33:07.860 --> 00:33:09.990
has been very important for me.

00:33:09.990 --> 00:33:12.780
But to couple that with
some of these other aspects

00:33:12.780 --> 00:33:15.570
of leading, executing, and driving teams,

00:33:15.570 --> 00:33:17.100
and the inspiration part,

00:33:17.100 --> 00:33:18.962
because in the end you're
gonna have hard things,

00:33:18.962 --> 00:33:21.900
there are gonna be hard days,
and projects, and challenges,

00:33:21.900 --> 00:33:23.910
but you need to have the
team with you, right,

00:33:23.910 --> 00:33:26.670
who are excited and
about coming in every day

00:33:26.670 --> 00:33:30.120
and working as partners to
try to solve these problems.

00:33:30.120 --> 00:33:34.443
That goes a hugely long way
into the overall success.

00:33:35.580 --> 00:33:39.153
So the very last chart before
we open it up for questions.

00:33:40.260 --> 00:33:41.280
You've heard about a lot of things

00:33:41.280 --> 00:33:43.140
that I like to talk about.

00:33:43.140 --> 00:33:44.523
I decided a few years ago during COVID

00:33:44.523 --> 00:33:46.560
when we were all stuck at home

00:33:46.560 --> 00:33:48.150
that I would start a blog

00:33:48.150 --> 00:33:50.160
and write about some of these topics,

00:33:50.160 --> 00:33:52.650
whether it's leadership,
and work-life balance,

00:33:52.650 --> 00:33:54.990
and what it looks like to be a woman

00:33:54.990 --> 00:33:58.410
in a largely male-dominated
hardware technology space,

00:33:58.410 --> 00:34:01.500
or science and innovation, et cetera.

00:34:01.500 --> 00:34:04.537
And one of my favorite things
is when someone will say,

00:34:04.537 --> 00:34:06.780
"Oh, I read that and I
agree, or I disagree,

00:34:06.780 --> 00:34:08.220
or I had a similar experience,

00:34:08.220 --> 00:34:09.540
or I'm also struggling with that,

00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:11.460
or my husband also does the laundry,"

00:34:11.460 --> 00:34:13.830
or whatever the story happens to be.

00:34:13.830 --> 00:34:16.140
So it's been a great conversation starter.

00:34:16.140 --> 00:34:18.990
And as Jeff said, my
husband also went to Yale,

00:34:18.990 --> 00:34:21.570
so he still happens to
be wearing a Yale shirt

00:34:21.570 --> 00:34:23.010
in this picture.

00:34:23.010 --> 00:34:24.930
He was in Solomon right near here,

00:34:24.930 --> 00:34:28.020
and we met working on The Yale Herald.

00:34:28.020 --> 00:34:31.560
So we have got a science and
he's a social studies teacher,

00:34:31.560 --> 00:34:33.960
and so we got different
sides of the brain.

00:34:33.960 --> 00:34:36.150
And we have three
children, two in college,

00:34:36.150 --> 00:34:37.680
one in middle school.

00:34:37.680 --> 00:34:40.650
And it's been an adventure, right?

00:34:40.650 --> 00:34:42.450
Lots of fun, lots of learning.

00:34:42.450 --> 00:34:47.070
And my favorite thing about
being in the technology space,

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:49.290
as I said, it's hard, but
it also, it's always...

00:34:49.290 --> 00:34:50.790
There's always something new to learn.

00:34:50.790 --> 00:34:51.780
There's always something new to learn

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:53.190
about the science and technology,

00:34:53.190 --> 00:34:55.470
but also in all those other aspects

00:34:55.470 --> 00:34:58.260
of leading projects and driving execution.

00:34:58.260 --> 00:35:01.500
And it's been a wild ride for me.

00:35:01.500 --> 00:35:04.140
I've got a chance to learn
over many, many years,

00:35:04.140 --> 00:35:07.410
and I look forward to
learning more going forward.

00:35:07.410 --> 00:35:09.880
So with that, I will open it up.

00:35:09.880 --> 00:35:13.047
(audience applauding)

00:35:16.800 --> 00:35:18.180
- Great, thank you so much, Kathryn.

00:35:18.180 --> 00:35:19.800
Okay, great, we'll get some seats.

00:35:19.800 --> 00:35:22.600
I also do the laundry, by the
way, just full disclosure.

00:35:25.080 --> 00:35:27.643
So I want to open it up for questions,

00:35:27.643 --> 00:35:30.513
but I have a couple questions
of my own, if I may.

00:35:31.860 --> 00:35:35.390
The first is Yale...

00:35:36.270 --> 00:35:39.090
prepares us in various
ways for various things.

00:35:39.090 --> 00:35:42.330
What would you say is
the thing you remember

00:35:42.330 --> 00:35:45.330
about your Yale experience
that has helped you the most,

00:35:45.330 --> 00:35:49.170
and what do you wish you had learned here?

00:35:49.170 --> 00:35:51.570
- Interesting question.
- That they missed.

00:35:51.570 --> 00:35:52.403
We missed.

00:35:55.050 --> 00:35:57.780
- So one of the most
pivotal experiences I had

00:35:57.780 --> 00:36:02.460
was as a leader on The Yale
Herald or the weekly newspaper.

00:36:02.460 --> 00:36:04.890
And not just because I met my husband,

00:36:04.890 --> 00:36:06.600
although that was also pivotal,

00:36:06.600 --> 00:36:09.600
but being in a leadership role

00:36:09.600 --> 00:36:11.190
with people who didn't have to...

00:36:11.190 --> 00:36:12.510
We weren't getting paid, right?

00:36:12.510 --> 00:36:15.720
Nobody had a job and was
really worrying about so much

00:36:15.720 --> 00:36:17.760
on their resume.

00:36:17.760 --> 00:36:20.010
But it was because people felt like

00:36:20.010 --> 00:36:21.660
they wanted to be a part of the team,

00:36:21.660 --> 00:36:25.950
and that they felt valued,
and that we all made it fun,

00:36:25.950 --> 00:36:30.360
but we also cared deeply about
the quality of the output.

00:36:30.360 --> 00:36:32.520
And I think that experience,

00:36:32.520 --> 00:36:34.200
perhaps more than anything else,

00:36:34.200 --> 00:36:35.520
is something that I took forward

00:36:35.520 --> 00:36:38.130
into other leadership roles that I had.

00:36:38.130 --> 00:36:40.320
And so that's something
I absolutely remember

00:36:40.320 --> 00:36:43.050
and just working with brilliant people

00:36:43.050 --> 00:36:46.314
and being in a community
of smart and motivated,

00:36:46.314 --> 00:36:49.350
ambitious but collaborative people.

00:36:49.350 --> 00:36:52.002
That's something I really
valued in my time at Yale,

00:36:52.002 --> 00:36:53.310
and I've looked for,

00:36:53.310 --> 00:36:55.410
and one of the things about IBM

00:36:55.410 --> 00:36:56.670
is you're working with people

00:36:56.670 --> 00:36:58.776
who are deeply smart, and motivated,

00:36:58.776 --> 00:37:01.410
and not so different perhaps
in the community here.

00:37:01.410 --> 00:37:04.170
So absolutely have enjoyed that.

00:37:04.170 --> 00:37:05.523
What do I wish?

00:37:06.660 --> 00:37:07.890
So I learned so much

00:37:07.890 --> 00:37:10.860
from a technical perspective
in the applied physics.

00:37:10.860 --> 00:37:12.600
Applied physics was, and I think still is,

00:37:12.600 --> 00:37:13.890
a fairly small department,

00:37:13.890 --> 00:37:16.380
and I was deliberate about that,

00:37:16.380 --> 00:37:18.240
and I stayed in an Applied Physics Program

00:37:18.240 --> 00:37:19.230
in graduate school as well

00:37:19.230 --> 00:37:22.110
because I liked that basic science,

00:37:22.110 --> 00:37:25.500
but also applying it
to real-world problems.

00:37:25.500 --> 00:37:30.093
I sought the industry
experience and reaching out.

00:37:30.930 --> 00:37:34.200
I would say I didn't feel
like there was a lot of...

00:37:34.200 --> 00:37:35.846
I got lucky that I have a professor

00:37:35.846 --> 00:37:37.680
who connected me to somebody,

00:37:37.680 --> 00:37:41.730
but I think the opportunity to
learn about what kind of jobs

00:37:41.730 --> 00:37:44.910
and what kind of opportunities
exist out there in the world.

00:37:44.910 --> 00:37:46.260
So like what you're doing

00:37:46.260 --> 00:37:47.790
and inviting people in to share that,

00:37:47.790 --> 00:37:48.990
I think that's great.

00:37:48.990 --> 00:37:52.170
I didn't feel like I had a
lot of that when I was here

00:37:52.170 --> 00:37:55.200
and I couldn't really imagine
what life would be like after,

00:37:55.200 --> 00:37:57.643
and so I just kept going
and then it worked out.

00:37:57.643 --> 00:38:00.330
- Mm-hmm, yeah, it is a true unicorn here,

00:38:00.330 --> 00:38:01.590
the applied physics major.

00:38:01.590 --> 00:38:02.490
I don't know there...

00:38:02.490 --> 00:38:04.350
How many applied physics
majors are there in the office?

00:38:04.350 --> 00:38:06.210
- Anyone?
- Ah, there we go, okay.

00:38:06.210 --> 00:38:07.923
Okay, great.

00:38:09.660 --> 00:38:11.640
Yeah, I mean that's really interesting.

00:38:11.640 --> 00:38:16.200
So we wrestle, and the
institution wrestles, I think,

00:38:16.200 --> 00:38:19.710
with the question of the kinds of how much

00:38:19.710 --> 00:38:22.650
we should broaden the
training of undergraduates

00:38:22.650 --> 00:38:25.170
to include the things that you mentioned

00:38:25.170 --> 00:38:26.700
as these really key skills

00:38:26.700 --> 00:38:29.250
that you are developing
kind of on the job, right?

00:38:29.250 --> 00:38:34.250
So do you have a sense of if
we were to think about new ways

00:38:34.800 --> 00:38:35.940
to develop the curriculum

00:38:35.940 --> 00:38:37.800
that would prepare people
for some of the things

00:38:37.800 --> 00:38:41.493
you didn't learn here but
maybe wish you could have,

00:38:42.540 --> 00:38:44.910
is there kind of a list of key things

00:38:44.910 --> 00:38:47.760
that you would suggest we think about?

00:38:47.760 --> 00:38:49.440
- I think there are.

00:38:49.440 --> 00:38:52.410
I think there are frameworks and models

00:38:52.410 --> 00:38:54.903
for some of the things
that I've talked about.

00:38:56.880 --> 00:38:59.340
And I think there's an opportunity
to both learn about them,

00:38:59.340 --> 00:39:02.130
sort of the book learning of
it, but also to practice it.

00:39:02.130 --> 00:39:04.500
And so I know you have
experiential courses,

00:39:04.500 --> 00:39:07.170
leading teams and projects.

00:39:07.170 --> 00:39:11.400
I'd say there's very little
of having a hard problem

00:39:11.400 --> 00:39:14.670
and going off and thinking
about that by yourself in a room

00:39:14.670 --> 00:39:18.720
that I've experienced in
the professional world.

00:39:18.720 --> 00:39:21.090
A lot of this is team-based learning.

00:39:21.090 --> 00:39:22.770
It's figuring things out together.

00:39:22.770 --> 00:39:25.620
It's taking advantage of all
these different perspectives.

00:39:25.620 --> 00:39:30.390
So I think finding ways
to get that experience

00:39:30.390 --> 00:39:31.350
while you're a student

00:39:31.350 --> 00:39:33.090
so it doesn't feel so foreign

00:39:33.090 --> 00:39:35.373
when you get into the working world.

00:39:36.270 --> 00:39:37.593
- Yeah, yeah, I agree.

00:39:38.610 --> 00:39:39.840
And then I guess I have one more

00:39:39.840 --> 00:39:41.760
and then I'm gonna open it up.

00:39:41.760 --> 00:39:46.760
So we are really eager to
bring kind of like this,

00:39:47.040 --> 00:39:48.810
bring some of the
knowledge, and experience,

00:39:48.810 --> 00:39:52.170
and even the sort of technology
and technology awareness

00:39:52.170 --> 00:39:54.420
that exists in industry

00:39:54.420 --> 00:39:56.370
into how we think about
teaching our students,

00:39:56.370 --> 00:39:57.990
how we think about collaborating

00:39:57.990 --> 00:40:00.000
in terms of our research labs.

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:03.090
What do you see as the
sort of positives of that,

00:40:03.090 --> 00:40:04.860
but also some of the pitfalls

00:40:04.860 --> 00:40:06.630
that universities need to be aware of

00:40:06.630 --> 00:40:10.950
as we sort of embrace technology
that exists in industry?

00:40:10.950 --> 00:40:14.190
- Yeah, I think there's huge
value of bringing it in,

00:40:14.190 --> 00:40:15.210
and making it relevant,

00:40:15.210 --> 00:40:18.660
and seeing kind of this real-life,
tangible what's going on,

00:40:18.660 --> 00:40:20.310
and how do you get your arms around it.

00:40:20.310 --> 00:40:25.310
But I think the pitfall is
you wanna get so enamored

00:40:25.680 --> 00:40:27.420
with one particular technology, right?

00:40:27.420 --> 00:40:32.040
ChatGPTx is not gonna
be the end-all, be-all

00:40:32.040 --> 00:40:33.480
for the rest of your careers.

00:40:33.480 --> 00:40:37.620
It's more about how do you understand the:

00:40:37.620 --> 00:40:39.330
What led to that innovation?

00:40:39.330 --> 00:40:42.836
What are the key components of it?

00:40:42.836 --> 00:40:45.990
How do you understand when
new technology emerges?

00:40:45.990 --> 00:40:47.160
How do you take advantage of it?

00:40:47.160 --> 00:40:49.320
Because there'll be next year,

00:40:49.320 --> 00:40:50.970
five years from now, 10 years from now,

00:40:50.970 --> 00:40:52.530
it'll be something else.

00:40:52.530 --> 00:40:55.830
And so it's really, I think
it's important to be relevant,

00:40:55.830 --> 00:40:56.940
and to be connected,

00:40:56.940 --> 00:41:00.480
but not to get so enamored
by whatever it is today.

00:41:00.480 --> 00:41:02.850
Because the technology
in the business world

00:41:02.850 --> 00:41:04.020
is changing so fast.

00:41:04.020 --> 00:41:06.993
It will certainly be
different as you progress.

00:41:08.340 --> 00:41:10.953
- Good. Okay, any questions
from the audience?

00:41:14.370 --> 00:41:15.690
Yeah, right here?

00:41:15.690 --> 00:41:18.690
- [Audience 1] Sure.
- Oh, we have a mic 'cause...

00:41:18.690 --> 00:41:19.523
There you go.

00:41:20.640 --> 00:41:23.790
- When you made the transition
from a sort of research,

00:41:23.790 --> 00:41:26.910
sort of PhD role, to
a more businessy role,

00:41:26.910 --> 00:41:29.310
I guess there were a lot of
skills, as you sort of said,

00:41:29.310 --> 00:41:32.550
that you sort of maybe
didn't have or had to learn.

00:41:32.550 --> 00:41:33.510
To what extent did you feel

00:41:33.510 --> 00:41:36.120
like you had to be really
proactive about going out

00:41:36.120 --> 00:41:37.050
and learning those

00:41:37.050 --> 00:41:40.290
versus really learning them on the job

00:41:40.290 --> 00:41:42.690
or just sort of like using
the sort of experiences

00:41:42.690 --> 00:41:43.523
to guide you?

00:41:45.030 --> 00:41:47.820
- I did a lot of learning
on the job, I would say,

00:41:47.820 --> 00:41:51.273
and I think that is pretty typical.

00:41:52.500 --> 00:41:54.660
Certainly at IBM, I can't
speak to every other company,

00:41:54.660 --> 00:41:57.153
but I think in tech that's pretty typical.

00:41:58.800 --> 00:42:00.060
In technology,

00:42:00.060 --> 00:42:02.820
even people outside of, say,
the research and development,

00:42:02.820 --> 00:42:05.880
we don't have a huge number of pure

00:42:05.880 --> 00:42:09.627
sort of MBA business management leaders.

00:42:09.627 --> 00:42:11.610
The fact that the CEO of IBM

00:42:11.610 --> 00:42:14.010
is a PhD electrical engineer, right?

00:42:14.010 --> 00:42:15.990
Not an MBA, not a...

00:42:15.990 --> 00:42:17.850
I mean, he is a super smart businessman,

00:42:17.850 --> 00:42:21.720
but his technical training
is in engineering.

00:42:21.720 --> 00:42:24.420
And that's sort of the
ethos of the company,

00:42:24.420 --> 00:42:28.050
and so it wasn't like I had
to go get an MBA or I had to,

00:42:28.050 --> 00:42:32.280
and I took various classes
and I found value in that,

00:42:32.280 --> 00:42:35.040
but much of that was learned on the job.

00:42:35.040 --> 00:42:37.770
And in fact, my first management role,

00:42:37.770 --> 00:42:40.530
the first time I was
leading a team of people

00:42:40.530 --> 00:42:42.960
and they had to do performance reviews

00:42:42.960 --> 00:42:45.513
and be responsible for their success.

00:42:46.950 --> 00:42:49.800
As is typical, you get
promoted into those positions

00:42:49.800 --> 00:42:52.920
because you're good at
your other job, which I...

00:42:52.920 --> 00:42:55.170
This is something every
company out there does,

00:42:55.170 --> 00:42:56.887
and it's typically a mistake, but,

00:42:56.887 --> 00:42:59.070
"Hey, you're really good at
being a technical contributor.

00:42:59.070 --> 00:43:00.570
We'll make you a manager."

00:43:00.570 --> 00:43:01.410
And all of a sudden,

00:43:01.410 --> 00:43:03.120
you have people in management positions

00:43:03.120 --> 00:43:04.950
who have no business being
in management positions

00:43:04.950 --> 00:43:08.340
because they may not actually be that good

00:43:08.340 --> 00:43:12.030
at the guidance of a team
or the leadership of a team.

00:43:12.030 --> 00:43:13.230
They may be just really,

00:43:13.230 --> 00:43:14.730
really good individual contributors.

00:43:14.730 --> 00:43:16.560
Now, some grow into it, right?

00:43:16.560 --> 00:43:20.460
Some actually embrace it
and want, but others don't,

00:43:20.460 --> 00:43:21.660
and then that's a disservice

00:43:21.660 --> 00:43:23.580
to the people in their
organization, right?

00:43:23.580 --> 00:43:25.560
They just suddenly now have somebody

00:43:25.560 --> 00:43:27.960
that is looking for the power or the...

00:43:27.960 --> 00:43:30.480
So for me, when I got that
first leadership position,

00:43:30.480 --> 00:43:32.670
I was actually super excited about it.

00:43:32.670 --> 00:43:35.580
I wanted that and I valued that,

00:43:35.580 --> 00:43:38.610
but I also knew that I
didn't have the experience.

00:43:38.610 --> 00:43:40.080
Suddenly, I was managing people

00:43:40.080 --> 00:43:44.760
who had many, many more years
of experience than I did,

00:43:44.760 --> 00:43:46.700
who had...

00:43:48.690 --> 00:43:50.340
Sometimes difficult to manage, right?

00:43:50.340 --> 00:43:52.110
Especially in the research community,

00:43:52.110 --> 00:43:55.533
notoriously sort of
independent-minded, as you can imagine.

00:43:56.640 --> 00:43:59.670
And I didn't feel like
the role of the managers

00:43:59.670 --> 00:44:02.010
or my role was particularly valued

00:44:02.010 --> 00:44:04.620
even amongst the leadership in research.

00:44:04.620 --> 00:44:06.270
That, like, I was disappointed by that.

00:44:06.270 --> 00:44:07.800
I said, "This is really important.

00:44:07.800 --> 00:44:08.970
I wanna do a great job at this."

00:44:08.970 --> 00:44:09.803
And they're like, "Yeah, yeah, yeah,

00:44:09.803 --> 00:44:10.812
just keep doing your research."

00:44:10.812 --> 00:44:12.270
That was how I felt.

00:44:12.270 --> 00:44:13.710
I don't think that was actually accurate,

00:44:13.710 --> 00:44:15.000
but that's how I felt.

00:44:15.000 --> 00:44:17.400
When I moved into other parts of IBM,

00:44:17.400 --> 00:44:20.310
particularly into development teams,

00:44:20.310 --> 00:44:24.330
the role of the manager,
it was super important.

00:44:24.330 --> 00:44:26.610
'Cause suddenly, you
couldn't get a project done.

00:44:26.610 --> 00:44:29.700
You couldn't deliver our
chip design on schedule,

00:44:29.700 --> 00:44:32.160
keeping your team motivated.

00:44:32.160 --> 00:44:35.760
If you didn't have managers
who were really equipped

00:44:35.760 --> 00:44:37.320
to lead these teams effectively,

00:44:37.320 --> 00:44:39.120
but also have the technical credibility

00:44:39.120 --> 00:44:40.980
that the team would listen,

00:44:40.980 --> 00:44:43.950
and so I really liked being a manager

00:44:43.950 --> 00:44:45.420
in the development organization

00:44:45.420 --> 00:44:49.230
because suddenly that
role was really important.

00:44:49.230 --> 00:44:51.540
And I was playing not only
a people management role,

00:44:51.540 --> 00:44:53.400
but also a project management role.

00:44:53.400 --> 00:44:56.040
So responsible for the
delivery of the project.

00:44:56.040 --> 00:44:57.740
And I found that really rewarding.

00:45:00.870 --> 00:45:01.703
- Yeah.

00:45:02.610 --> 00:45:04.170
Either way, yeah.

00:45:04.170 --> 00:45:05.490
Circle around.

00:45:05.490 --> 00:45:07.680
- [Audience 2] Did you ever
experience impostor syndrome?

00:45:07.680 --> 00:45:10.230
And if you did, how
did you deal with that?

00:45:10.230 --> 00:45:11.400
- Yeah, that's a great question.

00:45:11.400 --> 00:45:13.950
So impostor syndrome, for
those who haven't heard of it,

00:45:13.950 --> 00:45:17.077
is very common that people feel like,

00:45:17.077 --> 00:45:19.503
"Well, I'm not deserving.

00:45:20.370 --> 00:45:21.960
Why am I in this position?"

00:45:21.960 --> 00:45:26.960
So yes, most of us in in
leadership positions have.

00:45:29.010 --> 00:45:31.620
Particularly when I became the IBM CIO.

00:45:31.620 --> 00:45:34.370
So suddenly I was in a C-suite
position at the company,

00:45:35.640 --> 00:45:36.473
and I thought,

00:45:38.377 --> 00:45:39.210
"Why me?

00:45:39.210 --> 00:45:44.210
Right, why am I in this role?
Do I deserve to be here?"

00:45:44.640 --> 00:45:45.960
And it's very natural, right?

00:45:45.960 --> 00:45:47.400
It's, I think many of us,

00:45:47.400 --> 00:45:50.160
and it's particularly common among women,

00:45:50.160 --> 00:45:52.590
question whether you are deserving

00:45:52.590 --> 00:45:57.210
or whether you are up to the task.

00:45:57.210 --> 00:46:00.720
I think the biggest advice I have

00:46:00.720 --> 00:46:03.363
is that you're in it for a reason, right?

00:46:04.350 --> 00:46:07.820
Nobody gives you a job
like this to be nice.

00:46:10.027 --> 00:46:11.550
"Hey, Jeff, wouldn't it be nice?"

00:46:11.550 --> 00:46:13.500
I mean, It doesn't happen,
it just doesn't happen.

00:46:13.500 --> 00:46:16.890
It's too important to the
business, to the company.

00:46:16.890 --> 00:46:18.930
Nobody's getting token jobs.

00:46:18.930 --> 00:46:23.017
So you gotta bolster your
confidence a little bit to say,

00:46:23.017 --> 00:46:25.170
"Of course, it's deserved,"

00:46:25.170 --> 00:46:27.120
but then you have to earn it too, right?

00:46:27.120 --> 00:46:30.720
So for me, it's about,
"Okay, get over that,

00:46:30.720 --> 00:46:33.630
but then what do I need to do

00:46:33.630 --> 00:46:36.210
to make sure that I can
do a great job, right?

00:46:36.210 --> 00:46:38.250
What do I need to be effective?"

00:46:38.250 --> 00:46:40.320
And it doesn't mean knowing everything,

00:46:40.320 --> 00:46:43.350
or being the smartest in the
room, or knowing all aspects.

00:46:43.350 --> 00:46:45.750
I showed you that CIO portfolio.

00:46:45.750 --> 00:46:48.180
Look, I took the job, I
didn't even know that, right?

00:46:48.180 --> 00:46:50.610
I literally didn't know
what I was getting into.

00:46:50.610 --> 00:46:53.580
I had been in many other
parts of the company,

00:46:53.580 --> 00:46:57.090
but the reason why our CEO
decided to put me in the role

00:46:57.090 --> 00:47:00.840
was suddenly they had somebody
who had developed products,

00:47:00.840 --> 00:47:03.510
who knew the IBM organization,

00:47:03.510 --> 00:47:08.460
who understood how to lead large
teams and drive innovation.

00:47:08.460 --> 00:47:11.340
So I was coming with a
different set of experiences

00:47:11.340 --> 00:47:13.200
than my predecessor and others

00:47:13.200 --> 00:47:15.390
who had been in that space before.

00:47:15.390 --> 00:47:16.230
And I was focused on:

00:47:16.230 --> 00:47:17.460
How do I bring that,

00:47:17.460 --> 00:47:20.640
how do I bring that value
that I was selected for,

00:47:20.640 --> 00:47:22.290
but also how do I learn,

00:47:22.290 --> 00:47:23.820
how do I surround myself with people

00:47:23.820 --> 00:47:25.830
who can fill in some of those gaps

00:47:25.830 --> 00:47:28.290
and have expertise where I don't?

00:47:28.290 --> 00:47:31.020
And not be afraid to
ask a lot of questions.

00:47:31.020 --> 00:47:32.490
I ask a lot of questions

00:47:32.490 --> 00:47:34.380
and it's not because I'm
trying to test somebody else,

00:47:34.380 --> 00:47:38.340
but because I genuinely am
curious and I need to know,

00:47:38.340 --> 00:47:42.120
and I want us to explore and
figure things out together.

00:47:42.120 --> 00:47:44.100
So I think those are
all sort of techniques,

00:47:44.100 --> 00:47:47.040
but it's not unusual.

00:47:47.040 --> 00:47:50.160
I was just telling Jeff I
just got appointed to a...

00:47:50.160 --> 00:47:53.430
my first public company corporate board,

00:47:53.430 --> 00:47:56.592
and I said, "Well, this
board is so impressive.

00:47:56.592 --> 00:47:59.400
There are Nobel Prize winners

00:47:59.400 --> 00:48:01.620
and there are people who
have been leading all the..."

00:48:01.620 --> 00:48:05.415
And my first reaction is,
"Why do they want me on that?"

00:48:05.415 --> 00:48:06.480
(Kathryn chuckles)

00:48:06.480 --> 00:48:08.910
And so I still have it, I still have it,

00:48:08.910 --> 00:48:10.230
and I think it's only natural.

00:48:10.230 --> 00:48:11.940
I think it's good that you can continue

00:48:11.940 --> 00:48:12.780
to be somewhat humble

00:48:12.780 --> 00:48:15.280
and not think that you're
deserving of everything.

00:48:16.320 --> 00:48:17.760
On the other hand,

00:48:17.760 --> 00:48:21.007
kind of have to straighten
your spine and say,

00:48:21.007 --> 00:48:23.130
"I can do this, and I'll add value,

00:48:23.130 --> 00:48:24.030
and let me figure out

00:48:24.030 --> 00:48:25.740
how to ask a lot of questions and learn,

00:48:25.740 --> 00:48:28.721
but also figure out what
my unique value add is

00:48:28.721 --> 00:48:30.297
and lean into that."

00:48:32.640 --> 00:48:34.440
- [Audience 2] Yeah. Thank you. Thanks.

00:48:34.440 --> 00:48:35.640
- [Audience 3] Hi.

00:48:35.640 --> 00:48:38.580
It's related to the question
about transition from research

00:48:38.580 --> 00:48:41.250
to management and business jobs.

00:48:41.250 --> 00:48:45.180
I guess apart from skills
that are to be learned,

00:48:45.180 --> 00:48:48.810
what can you recommend on us
to prepare for it mentally?

00:48:48.810 --> 00:48:51.690
Is there a mindset change that 'cause...

00:48:51.690 --> 00:48:53.160
I guess I'm a PhD student,

00:48:53.160 --> 00:48:55.320
and as a PhD student, it's a lot of time,

00:48:55.320 --> 00:48:59.010
it's to make things
work right on the edge.

00:48:59.010 --> 00:48:59.843
- Once.

00:48:59.843 --> 00:49:01.230
- [Audience 3] And I take fun of that,

00:49:01.230 --> 00:49:04.800
but that's probably not the
correct spirit in management.

00:49:04.800 --> 00:49:07.260
So when I feel that
things can be done better,

00:49:07.260 --> 00:49:09.030
but there's compromise to make,

00:49:09.030 --> 00:49:11.520
when you feel this way,
do you feel uncomfortable?

00:49:11.520 --> 00:49:13.530
And do you think there's a mindset change

00:49:13.530 --> 00:49:16.410
that we can prepare ourselves?

00:49:16.410 --> 00:49:18.303
- Yeah, that's a great question.

00:49:20.700 --> 00:49:22.530
Yeah, it certainly is different.

00:49:22.530 --> 00:49:25.282
When you're doing research in a university

00:49:25.282 --> 00:49:29.703
or even in the research
community at a company like IBM,

00:49:30.630 --> 00:49:32.070
there's a bit of a luxury

00:49:32.070 --> 00:49:34.710
in that you're not looking necessarily

00:49:34.710 --> 00:49:37.410
at trying to build repeatable products,

00:49:37.410 --> 00:49:40.050
you're not looking at
trying to scale a solution.

00:49:40.050 --> 00:49:42.960
You're trying to typically
drive innovation,

00:49:42.960 --> 00:49:46.860
invent, demonstrate something is possible,

00:49:46.860 --> 00:49:48.930
and boy, that is exciting, right?

00:49:48.930 --> 00:49:51.030
Something that's never been done before.

00:49:51.030 --> 00:49:53.160
You're trying to show for the first time

00:49:53.160 --> 00:49:55.260
and to demonstrate that value.

00:49:55.260 --> 00:49:58.770
So there's so much great
experience from that,

00:49:58.770 --> 00:49:59.700
and not everybody can do it.

00:49:59.700 --> 00:50:00.990
Obviously, there are people who just,

00:50:00.990 --> 00:50:02.643
they're not programmed that way.

00:50:03.750 --> 00:50:07.170
They're not as excited by this invention,

00:50:07.170 --> 00:50:09.370
and this innovation,
and this brand-newness.

00:50:10.320 --> 00:50:12.870
But it is different when
you move into a space

00:50:12.870 --> 00:50:17.870
where suddenly you're trying
to deliver something at scale,

00:50:18.150 --> 00:50:21.450
where repeatability, and reliability,

00:50:21.450 --> 00:50:24.960
and performance, and
security, and privacy,

00:50:24.960 --> 00:50:28.230
like all these things become paramount.

00:50:28.230 --> 00:50:30.990
And even if you have something
that's been demonstrated,

00:50:30.990 --> 00:50:32.520
it's amazing,

00:50:32.520 --> 00:50:35.280
it may not have any place over here.

00:50:35.280 --> 00:50:36.941
And that's true, right?

00:50:36.941 --> 00:50:38.820
So I think one of the
interesting things for me

00:50:38.820 --> 00:50:41.430
when I went back to the Research Division

00:50:41.430 --> 00:50:43.920
after having been outside
it for quite some time,

00:50:43.920 --> 00:50:46.950
I had a completely different
mindset than I had before.

00:50:46.950 --> 00:50:49.110
So now I'm saying, "Why
are we doing this?"

00:50:49.110 --> 00:50:51.090
In some cases, not everything.

00:50:51.090 --> 00:50:53.370
Like, "This is never
going to work, right?"

00:50:53.370 --> 00:50:56.340
And I had to check that too

00:50:56.340 --> 00:50:59.280
because part of the role of research

00:50:59.280 --> 00:51:00.840
is to explore avenues

00:51:00.840 --> 00:51:04.380
that may or may not be
commercially viable.

00:51:04.380 --> 00:51:07.290
So that's okay that some
portion of the portfolio

00:51:07.290 --> 00:51:08.790
is pushing the envelope.

00:51:08.790 --> 00:51:11.400
We can't always know where it will go.

00:51:11.400 --> 00:51:13.560
So you don't wanna predispose, or limit,

00:51:13.560 --> 00:51:17.160
or stop doing things just
because there's risk.

00:51:17.160 --> 00:51:19.050
But there has to be a spectrum,

00:51:19.050 --> 00:51:20.040
and that was one of the things

00:51:20.040 --> 00:51:21.860
that I was focused on in research

00:51:21.860 --> 00:51:25.290
is to look at a portfolio of projects,

00:51:25.290 --> 00:51:28.530
some that are in this riskier element,

00:51:28.530 --> 00:51:33.210
which is okay to not always
see the commercial viability

00:51:33.210 --> 00:51:34.260
in the early days,

00:51:34.260 --> 00:51:38.310
and some that have a lower
risk and more alignment

00:51:38.310 --> 00:51:42.300
to the product portfolio
commercialization and so on.

00:51:42.300 --> 00:51:44.310
And as long as you're
deliberate about that,

00:51:44.310 --> 00:51:45.480
and you acknowledge it,

00:51:45.480 --> 00:51:48.030
and you're clear-eyed,
I think that's fine.

00:51:48.030 --> 00:51:48.960
But there's a difference.

00:51:48.960 --> 00:51:50.721
And if you do everything over here,

00:51:50.721 --> 00:51:53.010
I don't think the community,

00:51:53.010 --> 00:51:55.590
I don't think a industrial
research lab will survive

00:51:55.590 --> 00:51:57.870
if everything is so far afield

00:51:57.870 --> 00:52:00.690
that they're a shot in the
dark and super high risk.

00:52:00.690 --> 00:52:04.470
But if everything in research
is so close to product

00:52:04.470 --> 00:52:07.470
that it's a guaranteed home run,

00:52:07.470 --> 00:52:08.820
then you're probably just an extension

00:52:08.820 --> 00:52:10.380
of the development team.

00:52:10.380 --> 00:52:13.607
And it's probably not truly the role

00:52:13.607 --> 00:52:15.930
that a research
organization needs to play.

00:52:15.930 --> 00:52:19.161
So that spectrum or portfolio,
I think, is really important.

00:52:19.161 --> 00:52:20.010
- [Moderator] One more.
- Great.

00:52:20.010 --> 00:52:21.310
We have time for one more.

00:52:23.640 --> 00:52:24.923
Oh, there we go.

00:52:25.810 --> 00:52:27.150
- [Audience 4] So I was curious,

00:52:27.150 --> 00:52:29.310
since you've been at
IBM, I guess, 24 years,

00:52:29.310 --> 00:52:31.080
and it seems relatively rare

00:52:31.080 --> 00:52:33.930
that maybe someone would
stay at one spot for so long,

00:52:33.930 --> 00:52:35.820
especially in tech.

00:52:35.820 --> 00:52:38.550
One, did you ever consider leaving IBM,

00:52:38.550 --> 00:52:41.940
and were there different
points in your career

00:52:41.940 --> 00:52:44.070
where you really kind of
checked in with yourself,

00:52:44.070 --> 00:52:45.660
or consulted mentors,

00:52:45.660 --> 00:52:48.900
or even just how you thought about

00:52:48.900 --> 00:52:50.900
kind of reaffirming the decision to stay

00:52:50.900 --> 00:52:54.420
and what value you
might've actually derived

00:52:54.420 --> 00:52:57.240
from actually being in one
spot instead of moving around?

00:52:57.240 --> 00:52:59.670
- Yeah, really good question.

00:52:59.670 --> 00:53:03.270
It turns out that there are
not only a few people at IBM

00:53:03.270 --> 00:53:08.226
who stay 20, 25, 30, 40
years at the company.

00:53:08.226 --> 00:53:13.226
So it's not that unusual,
although at large,

00:53:13.680 --> 00:53:16.710
like the average tenure
for most people in jobs

00:53:16.710 --> 00:53:18.480
is four years in the country right now.

00:53:18.480 --> 00:53:22.023
So it certainly is strange on that side.

00:53:22.890 --> 00:53:25.890
If I were doing the
same thing for 24 years,

00:53:25.890 --> 00:53:27.870
I would've gotten bored
and left immediately.

00:53:27.870 --> 00:53:32.100
So the nice thing about a
company like IBM, it's so big,

00:53:32.100 --> 00:53:34.950
it has so many different
aspects to the company

00:53:34.950 --> 00:53:37.980
that I had the pleasure of
having a dozen different jobs

00:53:37.980 --> 00:53:40.050
over the course of 24 years.

00:53:40.050 --> 00:53:42.360
So that was a real benefit to me.

00:53:42.360 --> 00:53:44.280
And why, because I had a network

00:53:44.280 --> 00:53:45.720
of people that I had worked with,

00:53:45.720 --> 00:53:48.930
I understood the processes, I
know how to get things done,

00:53:48.930 --> 00:53:51.300
I understood our technology portfolio.

00:53:51.300 --> 00:53:53.850
I could take some of my
experiences in one area

00:53:53.850 --> 00:53:54.683
and move it to the other,

00:53:54.683 --> 00:53:56.370
and then all of a sudden
I had a network of people

00:53:56.370 --> 00:53:57.540
I could reach out to.

00:53:57.540 --> 00:54:00.840
So in development, suddenly
we had this technical problem,

00:54:00.840 --> 00:54:02.850
and I could call up my
friends, and colleagues,

00:54:02.850 --> 00:54:05.977
and former employees from
my research days and say,

00:54:05.977 --> 00:54:08.610
"Drive up to this development lab,

00:54:08.610 --> 00:54:10.470
we need your help," and so on.

00:54:10.470 --> 00:54:13.800
So I think that there were
sort of intangible benefits

00:54:13.800 --> 00:54:15.780
of being at the same company,

00:54:15.780 --> 00:54:16.950
even though I had the chance

00:54:16.950 --> 00:54:19.050
to do all these different things.

00:54:19.050 --> 00:54:20.190
I was deliberate

00:54:20.190 --> 00:54:22.170
about going after doing
different things, though,

00:54:22.170 --> 00:54:26.820
I mean, I would say there
are not just a few people

00:54:26.820 --> 00:54:29.640
who come to IBM Research
and stay at IBM Research,

00:54:29.640 --> 00:54:30.473
and it's a large...

00:54:30.473 --> 00:54:33.150
3,000 researchers, it's a large community,

00:54:33.150 --> 00:54:35.250
but there are 280,000
people at the company,

00:54:35.250 --> 00:54:37.500
so it's a much larger company.

00:54:37.500 --> 00:54:42.500
And it was the most surprising
change people said to me was,

00:54:43.237 --> 00:54:44.997
"Why are you leaving Research?"

00:54:45.887 --> 00:54:47.640
And I'd been there about six years,

00:54:47.640 --> 00:54:49.140
and I made a deliberate choice

00:54:49.140 --> 00:54:50.550
to go learn about what
the rest of the company.

00:54:50.550 --> 00:54:52.590
And that's what allowed
me to kind keep learning

00:54:52.590 --> 00:54:54.330
and keep having these
different experiences,

00:54:54.330 --> 00:54:57.333
which, for me, was what
kind of kept it interesting.

00:54:58.590 --> 00:55:02.820
- Great. Can I sound you
out on one last topic-

00:55:02.820 --> 00:55:04.653
- Sure.
- Before we adjourn?

00:55:06.150 --> 00:55:07.800
You mentioned one of your blog posts

00:55:07.800 --> 00:55:09.420
was about what it's like to be a woman

00:55:09.420 --> 00:55:13.020
in a sort of male-dominated
technology field.

00:55:13.020 --> 00:55:16.167
And I just wonder if you
could give us a sense of that

00:55:16.167 --> 00:55:19.473
and some pointers perhaps
for folks in our audience?

00:55:20.310 --> 00:55:21.900
- Yeah, there's no easy answers.

00:55:21.900 --> 00:55:24.900
Unfortunately, not enough
has changed, in my opinion.

00:55:24.900 --> 00:55:27.030
It wasn't so unusual
when I was back at Yale

00:55:27.030 --> 00:55:31.650
to be among a few of the women
in applied physics classes

00:55:31.650 --> 00:55:32.910
or in engineering classes.

00:55:32.910 --> 00:55:36.870
And that continued in my
PhD program at Stanford,

00:55:36.870 --> 00:55:39.090
that continued when I joined IBM Research.

00:55:39.090 --> 00:55:40.980
There was never a line
at the women's bathroom,

00:55:40.980 --> 00:55:42.810
so there's benefits.

00:55:42.810 --> 00:55:44.010
That continued when I moved

00:55:44.010 --> 00:55:45.479
into hardware development, right?

00:55:45.479 --> 00:55:47.400
Working in the semiconductor world,

00:55:47.400 --> 00:55:49.440
working in the systems development,

00:55:49.440 --> 00:55:51.720
and then even in the
product management side

00:55:51.720 --> 00:55:52.590
of that hardware.

00:55:52.590 --> 00:55:57.120
So hardware tends,
still, to be less diverse

00:55:57.120 --> 00:56:01.650
than other aspects of
engineering and applied science.

00:56:01.650 --> 00:56:03.330
So my disappointment

00:56:03.330 --> 00:56:06.570
is that not enough has
changed in the last 30 years

00:56:06.570 --> 00:56:08.043
as I would've hoped.

00:56:09.720 --> 00:56:13.170
The good news is I think
there's a lot more awareness

00:56:13.170 --> 00:56:18.170
that we need to tap into a
larger segment of our population

00:56:19.200 --> 00:56:24.180
for a lot of the important
innovation and opportunities.

00:56:24.180 --> 00:56:27.000
They're lucrative jobs,
they're exciting challenges.

00:56:27.000 --> 00:56:28.530
We want all diverse perspective.

00:56:28.530 --> 00:56:30.240
There's so many good reasons.

00:56:30.240 --> 00:56:31.740
There's truly a business imperative,

00:56:31.740 --> 00:56:34.830
not just it's the right thing to do,

00:56:34.830 --> 00:56:38.493
but again, we're still not
seeing such a dramatic change.

00:56:39.360 --> 00:56:42.900
I didn't experience overt
discrimination or bias.

00:56:42.900 --> 00:56:43.733
I really didn't.

00:56:45.270 --> 00:56:48.240
There were times where I felt awkward

00:56:48.240 --> 00:56:52.290
or I felt just disappointed

00:56:52.290 --> 00:56:54.720
that I was the only woman in the room

00:56:54.720 --> 00:56:58.140
and sometimes I was the leader in the room

00:56:58.140 --> 00:56:59.940
as the only woman, which can again,

00:56:59.940 --> 00:57:03.030
feel a little bit, I'd say uncomfortable,

00:57:03.030 --> 00:57:06.183
but you get used to it, I'd
say, a little bit over time.

00:57:07.590 --> 00:57:10.890
My experience is just to not
be afraid to sort of speak up

00:57:10.890 --> 00:57:13.410
when I need to,

00:57:13.410 --> 00:57:16.620
even if that takes a
little bit of practice,

00:57:16.620 --> 00:57:18.840
and then speak up for
others as well, right,

00:57:18.840 --> 00:57:21.150
when I see that there are women

00:57:21.150 --> 00:57:24.330
that maybe are sitting not at the table,

00:57:24.330 --> 00:57:27.030
or are not being heard.

00:57:27.030 --> 00:57:29.490
Stereotypically, but actually in practice,

00:57:29.490 --> 00:57:31.860
I have seen plenty of times
where a woman will say something

00:57:31.860 --> 00:57:33.300
and it's not really recognized,

00:57:33.300 --> 00:57:35.167
and a man will repeat
it and everyone says,

00:57:35.167 --> 00:57:37.290
"Oh, what a good idea!"

00:57:37.290 --> 00:57:42.290
And I will often say, "We
heard that five minutes ago.

00:57:42.488 --> 00:57:43.890
It was that good of idea,

00:57:43.890 --> 00:57:46.350
let's make sure we go back to so-and-so

00:57:46.350 --> 00:57:48.360
and hear what other ideas she has."

00:57:48.360 --> 00:57:50.640
So I think it's on all of us,

00:57:50.640 --> 00:57:53.310
but particularly for those who
are in leadership positions,

00:57:53.310 --> 00:57:57.300
to look out for circumstances

00:57:57.300 --> 00:58:00.600
where traditionally the woman
gets asked to take the notes,

00:58:00.600 --> 00:58:01.800
or send out the agenda,

00:58:01.800 --> 00:58:05.850
or do the things that are
the extra work when...

00:58:05.850 --> 00:58:07.920
That's reasonable sometimes,

00:58:07.920 --> 00:58:09.900
but that those sorts of responsibilities

00:58:09.900 --> 00:58:10.733
should be passed around.

00:58:10.733 --> 00:58:13.020
So as a leader, I think
there's more we can all do

00:58:13.020 --> 00:58:14.940
to call that out

00:58:14.940 --> 00:58:19.593
and then help to move things
forward in a positive way.

00:58:20.790 --> 00:58:22.260
- Good, well, Kathryn,

00:58:22.260 --> 00:58:24.600
thank you so much for
being here with us today,

00:58:24.600 --> 00:58:26.190
you're a tribute to Yale.

00:58:26.190 --> 00:58:28.260
And we're very pleased

00:58:28.260 --> 00:58:30.586
you're able to share
your story with us here.

00:58:30.586 --> 00:58:32.130
And thank you all for coming.

00:58:32.130 --> 00:58:35.704
Look forward to more Dean's
lectures in the future.

00:58:35.704 --> 00:58:37.073
Join us again.

00:58:37.073 --> 00:58:40.240
(audience applauding)

00:58:42.711 --> 00:58:45.461
(pensive music)

